WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:05.033
LaGuardia? 

00:00:12.366 --> 00:00:14.900
Today I'm traveling from 
New York to Chicago

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.266
on... this plane.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.466
It's an Airbus A319. 

00:00:18.466 --> 00:00:21.600
And I just so happened to have 
the blueprints or

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.433
Aircraft Characteristics, Airport and 
Maintenance Planning documents...

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:26.600
in my possession.

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.100
As you can see here: economy class

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.400
which is where I'll be sitting, 
has a seat pitch between 28

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.666
and 30 inches.

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:36.533
Seat pitch is the fancy term used 
to describe the distance

00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:38.200
between where your seat begins

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.900
and the seat in front of you ends.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.400
It's also colloquially known as 
legroom, for obvious reasons.

00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:45.866
Normally there's not enough.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.766
There’s actually an appropriate amount of space.

00:00:47.766 --> 00:00:51.000
Every time it’s like riding 
with seats at the knees. 

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.000
It could be better.

00:00:52.666 --> 00:00:55.100
Airplane seats weren't always 
designed this way.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.000
So why have they changed?

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.633
And how much legroom have we lost 
in the process?

00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:01.466
I'm going to try and find out.

00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:08.866
I'm 5’9” (175 cm)

00:01:08.866 --> 00:01:12.333
which according to Google, 
is precisely average.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.733
When I'm sitting down 
in an upright position

00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:16.100
My buttock-to-knee length...

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.400
which, before you harass me
in the comments

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.866
is the official measurements
that the FAA use in its testing

00:01:21.866 --> 00:01:23.866
ends up being about 20 inches.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.100
And when I got on this plane...

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.500
I ended up being fairly comfortable

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.433
because I have room between my knee 
and the seat in front of me. 

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.166
What’s that look like?
Five, six inches?

00:01:34.166 --> 00:01:36.366
Yeah... thereabouts.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:37.966
That lovely voice 
that you just heard

00:01:37.966 --> 00:01:39.833
is my new Australian friend:
Chris.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.500
G’day mate, y’know?

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.100
He and Brian, the two sweetest guys 
in the world

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.366
who I had the privilege of 
sitting next to

00:01:46.366 --> 00:01:49.266
did not have the same spacious feeling 
that I did.

00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:51.100
There's not enough room for me

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.866
because I'm a bit around six foot.

00:01:53.166 --> 00:01:54.533
Bit over six foot.

00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:56.633
And it’s hard for me to fit. 

00:01:56.733 --> 00:01:58.000
I'm not that big of a guy.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.300
I'm 5’10”.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.900
It's pretty, pretty crowded back here.

00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:03.900
And that might be because 
we have different

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.633
buttock-to-knee ratios.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.366
Like the space between Brian's knee
and the chair in front of him

00:02:09.366 --> 00:02:10.233
was just...

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.200
About three inches there.

00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:14.266
And Chris’s was...

00:02:14.266 --> 00:02:16.100
I can’t out a knife in there! 

00:02:16.566 --> 00:02:17.766
I don’t need to measure. 

00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:21.133
When I watch movies that...

00:02:21.233 --> 00:02:23.566
take place in 50s, 60s, 70s...

00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:27.066
everything seems so much more 
spacious and luxurious.

00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:28.733
And it's true.

00:02:28.766 --> 00:02:29.933
Take the DC-3

00:02:29.933 --> 00:02:33.333
Which American Airlines began using in 1936.

00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:35.800
AA’s president at the time, 
C.R. Smith

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.866
called it “The first airplane in the world

00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:40.500
that can make money just by 
hauling passengers.”

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.700
And it was used for decades.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.333
It seated 28 people.

00:02:44.333 --> 00:02:48.000
And according to this 1957 
Quebecair diagram

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.233
it had a luxurious seat pitch 
of 39 inches...

00:02:51.633 --> 00:02:55.266
which is like ten more inches 
than I had on my plane to Chicago.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.866
In the late 60s, 
Boeing’s 737 took over.

00:02:59.066 --> 00:03:01.633
And it became one of the most 
well-known and successful

00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:03.300
aircrafts of all time.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.033
Iterations on the plane are still used regularly today.

00:03:07.666 --> 00:03:11.833
These 2005 737 layout documents 
show that economy seats

00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:15.666
got anywhere from 30 to 34 inches 
in seat pitch.

00:03:15.666 --> 00:03:20.166
Today, the biggest competitor to the 737 
is the Airbus A320...

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:23.666
which I flew on my way home 
rom Chicago to NYC.

00:03:24.433 --> 00:03:26.766
It's about twice as long as the DC-3

00:03:26.766 --> 00:03:29.033
and can fit about 180 passengers.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.666
And like most planes today...

00:03:31.966 --> 00:03:33.900
seat pitch varies 
within the aircraft.

00:03:34.266 --> 00:03:36.266
On the lowest end for economy...

00:03:36.366 --> 00:03:38.800
it hovers around 28 to 29 inches.

00:03:39.066 --> 00:03:41.100
But there are different tiers.

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:44.566
Like on my flight back 
from Chicago to New York

00:03:44.566 --> 00:03:48.333
we paid about $30 extra for a 
premium economy seat.

00:03:48.866 --> 00:03:50.700
For research purposes only.

00:03:51.033 --> 00:03:53.066
I took absolutely no pleasure in this.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.300
The people I sat next to 
weren't as chatty

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:57.800
as they were on the way to Chicago.

00:03:58.433 --> 00:03:59.300
-Good luck.
-Bye!

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.233
Miss you, Chris. 

00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:03.866
But I had way more legroom.

00:04:04.066 --> 00:04:06.000
Not only was I in Economy Plus

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.133
but I also ended up in the 
notoriously spacious exit row.

00:04:10.266 --> 00:04:12.766
While, yes, I do get extra legroom.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.400
I also have a ton of 
extra responsibility

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.700
in case this plane goes down.

00:04:17.533 --> 00:04:18.300
But anyways

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.766
instead of just six inches

00:04:19.766 --> 00:04:21.900
between the seat in front of me 
and my knee

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:23.433
I now had 14. 

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.233
And it was luxurious.

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:29.000
As if Vox had sprung for 
first class for me

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.733
it would have cost $100 more 
than Economy Plus.

00:04:32.233 --> 00:04:34.666
But these schematics show 
that I would have gotten

00:04:34.666 --> 00:04:37.066
39 inches of legroom...

00:04:37.333 --> 00:04:40.400
the same as the seats on the DC-3 
from back in the day.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:42.600
But instead...

00:04:42.733 --> 00:04:44.700
I was here in 
premium economy.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.066
As I sat there with 
no TV in front of me...

00:04:48.433 --> 00:04:51.200
I began to think about 
the economics of comfort.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:52.833
Way back in the day

00:04:52.833 --> 00:04:54.633
they didn't charge different prices

00:04:54.633 --> 00:04:58.866
based on whether you had a bulkhead seat
or a window or an aisle seat.

00:04:59.166 --> 00:05:00.366
That's Nicholas Rupp.

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:03.600
He coauthored this 2022 paper 
that examined

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.300
in-flight amenities by carriers provided 
by the US airlines. 

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:08.400
In the past

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.266
everything would be bundled together 
in your ticket price.

00:05:11.666 --> 00:05:13.666
You'd get a carry-on,
a bag...

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.333
you usually got to 
choose your seats.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.533
But in the early 2000s

00:05:17.533 --> 00:05:21.666
because of rising fuel costs and 
a slew of difficult world events

00:05:21.666 --> 00:05:23.633
events that made people 
less inclined to travel...

00:05:23.933 --> 00:05:27.300
airliners started to unbundle 
all of those things...

00:05:27.633 --> 00:05:30.533
seemingly starting with baggage fees 
and eventually

00:05:30.533 --> 00:05:33.133
leading to charging customers more 
for seat selection.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.666
Airlines have done a good job

00:05:34.666 --> 00:05:37.133
at figuring out 
what consumers prefer

00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:39.033
and then being able 
to extract

00:05:39.033 --> 00:05:41.033
additional payment out of consumers.

00:05:41.033 --> 00:05:43.500
Over the years, these extras 
have added up.

00:05:43.833 --> 00:05:45.633
JetBlue and Spirit, for example...

00:05:45.766 --> 00:05:47.866
have increased their 
overall revenues

00:05:47.866 --> 00:05:51.266
by several percentage points 
through these fees alone...

00:05:51.666 --> 00:05:54.000
and this has forced customers to decide

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.200
with their dollar what they want.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.600
I would never pay for more legroom.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:01.133
I'm just inherently going to try to buy 
the cheapest flight.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.200
I'm not going to like buy like a nicer seat
just for like leg room.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.900
If it weren’t so much more
I'd be willing to do that.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.633
I feel like people that are taller 
should get priority seating.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.166
It's not their choice to be tall.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.466
Flying isn't cheap.

00:06:13.966 --> 00:06:15.966
But if you zoom out and take a look

00:06:15.966 --> 00:06:18.933
at the Bureau of Transportation's 
historical statistics...

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:23.266
and adjust for inflation, you can see that 
compared to 1993...

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.500
domestic flights have actually 
gotten less expensive.

00:06:27.433 --> 00:06:30.700
Nick tells me that this is in part 
because of unbundling.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.800
And even most recently 
it's due to innovations

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.366
like being able to have thinner seats.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.733
Yeah, that means changing 
the design of the seats

00:06:40.733 --> 00:06:44.566
to literally take out padding and depth 
from your seat back.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.766
For many airlines, 
this space saving meant

00:06:47.766 --> 00:06:50.566
that they were able to add 
an extra row of seats.

00:06:50.866 --> 00:06:53.900
But most seats got an inch closer together.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.033
For my experience, the seats were 
pretty comfortable though.

00:06:57.166 --> 00:06:59.700
I think we will not go back in time

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.933
where they had one price for 
wherever you sat in coach.

00:07:03.933 --> 00:07:06.266
I believe what we're going to see

00:07:06.266 --> 00:07:09.300
is continue segmentation 
in the market

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.866
offering a variety different 
classes of products...

00:07:12.166 --> 00:07:15.300
and then allow the consumer 
to self-select.

00:07:16.666 --> 00:07:17.666
[PA system chimes]

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.033
As I disembarked from the plane

00:07:21.033 --> 00:07:23.033
I thought about the seats 
that I had been in...

00:07:23.133 --> 00:07:25.233
and all the people in the seats 
around me.

00:07:25.433 --> 00:07:28.100
And everyone I spoke to earlier that day.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.400
Like this one guy...

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:33.000
if there could be a standard 
that was less cramped...

00:07:33.433 --> 00:07:34.433
I'd be thrilled.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.100
In 2018, Congress sought 
a standard like this

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:41.333
by introducing 
the FAA Reauthorization Act...

00:07:41.566 --> 00:07:44.200
which in Section 577 called for

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.300
minimum dimensions for passenger seats...

00:07:46.566 --> 00:07:48.000
including seat pitch

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.933
as they are necessary for safety purposes.

00:07:51.333 --> 00:07:54.500
The bill was signed into law 
on October 5th, 2018

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:58.966
but as of 2023, there are still 
no regulations

00:07:58.966 --> 00:08:02.366
in part thanks to a March court decision 
that decided

00:08:02.366 --> 00:08:05.266
there wasn't enough clear
and indisputable evidence

00:08:05.266 --> 00:08:06.933
that small seats materially

00:08:06.933 --> 00:08:09.600
slow the exit of passengers 
in an emergency.

00:08:09.966 --> 00:08:12.200
Some, like Senator Tammy Duckworth

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.033
think that the evacuation studies 
need to be redone

00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:18.900
to include people with disabilities
or increased risk of injury...

00:08:19.033 --> 00:08:22.566
to more accurately reflect 
a real world evacuation.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:26.733
But safety and comfort aren't 
necessarily intertwined.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.366
Comfort wasn't considered in the last study

00:08:29.366 --> 00:08:33.266
and the word doesn't show up at all 
in section 577.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:36.633
For now, seat pitch 
is still up to airlines...

00:08:36.966 --> 00:08:39.500
and comfort still comes at a cost.

00:08:40.333 --> 00:08:43.300
Once on my honeymoon, 
I got upgraded to first class...

00:08:43.833 --> 00:08:45.266
so it was quite nice.

00:08:46.033 --> 00:08:46.900
You can enjoy that

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:50.200
but I'm not willing to pay that
on a regular basis.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:55.933
On both my flights

00:08:55.933 --> 00:08:57.533
I was lucky enough to not have 
anybody I sat behind

00:08:57.533 --> 00:08:59.433
recline their seats...

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.500
even though that's not necessarily 
impacting legroom...

00:09:02.666 --> 00:09:05.033
it definitely makes me feel 
more claustrophobic.

00:09:05.566 --> 00:09:06.533
What about you?

00:09:06.533 --> 00:09:09.000
Are you pro- or anti-plane seat reclining?

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:10.600
Let us know in the comments below.

